<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Saint Lucia</h1><p class="st-meta">1979 CE – present · capital Castries</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Windward island independent since 1979, a parliamentary monarchy known for its Pitons, its Kwéyòl culture and two Nobel laureates.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-saint-lucia">Colony of Saint Lucia</a> became independent on 22 February 1979, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-compton">John Compton</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-workers-party-saint-lucia">United Workers Party</a> as prime minister and the British monarch as head of state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Politics has alternated between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-workers-party-saint-lucia">United Workers Party</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucia-labour-party">Saint Lucia Labour Party</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenny-anthony">Kenny Anthony</a> led governments from 1997. Bananas, sold under preferential access to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, were the economic backbone until the 1990s, when the end of European preferences forced a turn to tourism, which now dominates. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pitons-saint-lucia">Pitons</a> were declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2004. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derek-walcott">Derek Walcott</a>, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castries">Castries</a>, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1992, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-arthur-lewis">Arthur Lewis</a>'s economics prize in 1979.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Saint Lucia remains a parliamentary democracy and Commonwealth realm. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-j-pierre">Philip J. Pierre</a> of the Labour Party became prime minister in 2021, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucian-creole">Kwéyòl</a>, French place names and Catholic traditions continue to shape national identity.</p></div><aside 